            <ArticleTitle>Retinal embolism following percutaneous femoral cerebral angiography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>49-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 35-year-old female without evidence of atherosclerotic vascular disease underwent percutaneous femoral cerebral angiography. Perifoveal retinal infarction by exogenous arteriolar emboli produced a permanent absolute pericentral scotoma. Strict adherence to meticulous angiographic technique may prevent such an angiographic complication.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
